<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Exploring Our Global Community – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring Our Global Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Can I Learn From Other Cultures?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write formal and informal letters on general topics. Interactive: Write different types of messages to coordinate group projects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a formal letter requesting information about a cultural exchange or travel program (e.g., "I am writing to inquire about the requirements for your cultural travel program. Could you please provide information on the application process and schedule?") Can write messages to coordinate logistics for a group project focused on cultural exploration (e.g., "Hi everyone, just confirming that we'll meet at 9 a.m. on Saturday to discuss travel details. Please let me know if you have any specific preferences for the trip.") Can write informal letters sharing cultural experiences with a friend (e.g., "Hey! I had an amazing time in Emberá-Wounaan. I learned so much about their traditional jewelry.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in a quick "Dream Trip" brainstorming activity. The teacher asks students to quickly jot down 3-5 places they would *dream* of visiting and one unique *cultural experience* they imagine having there. Students are encouraged to use the sentence starter: "If I could go anywhere, I would visit..."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes an example sentence on the board: "If I could visit a remote *destination*, I *would explore* the *breathtaking* Amazon rainforest. I *would learn* about its *diverse traditions*." The teacher models the pronunciation of the second conditional, focusing on vowel reduction in "could" (/kəd/) and "would" (/wʊd/), and emphasizes stress on multisyllabic words like "des-ti-NA-tion," "breathtaking," and "tra-DI-tions."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we talking about a real trip you took or a hypothetical one?" (Hypothetical)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'breathtaking' mean something ugly or incredibly stunning?" (Incredibly stunning / beautiful)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides students with two short example texts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A formal email requesting information about a "year-long Exchange Program" to a "magnificent destination."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">An informal message from a student to a friend, narrating an "unforgettable adventure" in Emberá-Wounaan, sharing about "traditional jewelry" and "diverse culture."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Can understand the main points of short, clear, simple messages and texts related to topics of personal interest and use this understanding as a model for their own writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the two texts and answer gist questions, focusing on purpose and audience for effective writing.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main purpose of the first text (email)?" (To request information about an Exchange Program, specifically its requirements and schedule).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who is the second text (message) written to?" (A friend).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of experience is being shared in the informal message?" (An unforgettable cultural adventure and learning about traditions).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences and short paragraphs using Second Conditional for hypothetical travel, Superlatives for comparisons, and Past Continuous for narrating experiences, incorporating target vocabulary related to travel and culture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion & Expansion (5 min):</b> Students complete sentences focusing on grammar and vocabulary. The teacher provides sentence starters or gaps, and students fill them in, ensuring correct grammar and appropriate vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: "If I ________ (verb: travel) to Japan, I ________ (verb: experience) the most ________ (adj: unique) *traditions*." (Expected: *traveled, would experience, unique*)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "While they ________ (verb: explore) the ancient city, they ________ (verb: discover) many ________ (adj: breathtaking) *wonders*." (Expected: *were exploring, were discovering, breathtaking*)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Paragraph Transformation (7 min):</b> Students are given short factual statements about cultural experiences and rewrite them into a descriptive paragraph. They must incorporate at least one Second Conditional sentence, one Superlative phrase, and two Past Continuous clauses. They are prompted to use at least five target vocabulary words (e.g., *adventure, destination, diverse, understand, broaden, enhance*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example statement: "Panama has many islands. Visiting the Guna Yala islands was very interesting. I learned about their customs."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student task: Transform this into a paragraph for a travel blog, adding reflections or hypothetical ideas.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check & Peer Feedback (3 min):</b> Students exchange their transformed paragraphs from activity 2 with a partner. Using a simple checklist, they collaboratively verify the correct use of Second Conditional, Superlatives, Past Continuous, and the inclusion of target vocabulary. This activity also fosters sociolinguistic competence by encouraging respectful discussion and feedback.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students engage in a comprehensive writing task that combines different forms of communication within the theme "What Can I Learn From Other Cultures?" and scenario "Exploring Our Global Community."</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Formal Letter/Email (80-100 words):</b> Students write a formal email to a "Cultural Exchange Program" organization (e.g., "Global Perspectives Exchange"). They must inquire about the requirements for a "year-long Exchange Program" to a specific "destination" (e.g., Japan, where they hope to *learn* about its *unique traditions* and *enhance* their global *understanding*). They should use formal register and include questions about the "application process," "schedule," and potential "benefits" for developing "skills" and *broadening perspectives*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Informal Message/Letter (50-70 words):</b> Students write an informal message or short letter to a friend. They can either:</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">a) Share a hypothetical "dream trip" using the Second Conditional (e.g., "Hey! If I *could travel* anywhere, I *would visit* the *breathtaking pyramids* in Egypt! I *would explore* its ancient *culture* and *experience* something truly *unforgettable*.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">b) Narrate a past *unforgettable adventure* using the Past Continuous (e.g., "Hi! I had an *amazing time* in Emberá-Wounaan recently! While I *was hiking* through the *natural* beauty, I *was learning* so much about their *traditional jewelry* and *diverse* way of life. It was a fantastic *experience*!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Group Coordination Messages (2-3 short messages):</b> Students draft 2-3 short messages, as if to a group chat, to coordinate logistics for a hypothetical group project focused on "cultural exploration." These messages should include suggestions for a "meeting time" and "location," propose a "topic to explore" (e.g., "comparing traditions" from *different cultures*), and explicitly invite input from classmates, demonstrating sociolinguistic competence in leading group projects and ensuring everyone has input.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist or a simplified rubric to assess students' written products based on the evaluation criteria below.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their formal email, informal message/letter, and group coordination messages from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content & Task Completion:</b> All parts of the writing task are addressed (formal request, informal sharing/narration, coordination messages).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Organization & Cohesion:</b> Clear structure, logical flow, and appropriate paragraphing/message formatting.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct and appropriate use of Second Conditional, Superlatives, and Past Continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Appropriate and varied use of target vocabulary (e.g., *experience, diversity, adventure, destination, breathtaking, unique, understand, broaden, enhance*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Register & Tone:</b> Appropriate for both formal (email) and informal (message/chat) contexts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Readability:</b> Ideas are expressed clearly and are easy to understand.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging aspect of writing the formal email compared to the informal message?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List two new vocabulary words you successfully incorporated into your writing today."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did today's writing activities help you differentiate between formal and informal writing styles?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In what ways do you think exploring other cultures through writing can enhance your understanding and broaden your perspectives?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students effectively apply the grammatical structures (Second Conditional, Superlatives, Past Continuous) and target vocabulary in their varied writing tasks? Was the distinction between formal and informal register clear in their output?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more structured templates, specific vocabulary banks for different contexts) are needed for future writing tasks to further improve their fluency, accuracy, and confidence in expressing complex ideas about global cultures?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 5), we will move into Mediation and Integration, where we will consolidate all the skills (listening, reading, speaking, and writing) we've developed throughout this theme. Students will apply their understanding to a collaborative 21st Century Skills Project, such as summarizing information about a cultural exchange program or creating a multimedia presentation about a chosen cultural destination, mediating information for a specific audience."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>